.SH DESCRIPTION
A table draws what a program would otherwise draw row by row: the columns,
the header and the selection.
The program gives the number of rows, the column definitions and a
callback that fills the cells; it never paints a row, so the cursor looks
the same in every program of the suite.
Navigation is that of
.BR tui_listview_init (3).
.PP
A column is a
.I tui_table_col
with a
.IR title ,
a
.I width
in cells, a
.I weight
(0: fixed; greater: it shares what is left, and
.I width
is its minimum), an alignment, a
.I hide_below
terminal width under which it is dropped, a
.I sort
marker in the header (1 shows
.RB \(dq \(ua \(dq ,
\-1
.RB \(dq \(da \(dq )
and
.BR TUI_COL_CUT_START ,
which cuts the beginning of a cell that is too long, as an address wants.
Columns are one blank cell apart, after a gutter of two cells.
Text is cut by display width, at a character boundary, and the cut shows as
.RB \(dq \[u2026] \(dq .
.PP
The callbacks of the
.I tui_table_model
get an opaque row or cell to describe.
A cell is any number of spans:
.BR tui_table_cell_put ()
appends text in its own style (a status column of single glyphs, each in
its colour).
.BR tui_table_cell_prefix ()
is text that is never cut (a tree's glyphs, an indent): the rest is cut,
not it.
A row has a kind,
.B TUI_ROW_DATA
or
.BR TUI_ROW_SECTION ;
a section row is a heading with a rule across the whole width
.RB ( tui_table_row_text ())
and the selection skips it.
.BR tui_table_row_style ()
is a style under the whole row (a colour, dim); a cell's own style goes on
top.
.BR tui_table_row_mark ()
is one glyph beside the cursor, which stays visible on the selected row.
The row callback may be called at any time, also for a row that is not
drawn, and has no effect of its own.
Only the rows in view are asked for, so a table of tens of thousands of
rows costs a screenful.
.PP
The selected row has a dark blue background, the text keeping its own
colour, and a
.RB \(dq > \(dq
in the first column (policies/tui-interface-model.md).
Text without a colour is bright white on it, so that it reads on a light
terminal theme too, and one of the eight basic colours is shown in its
bright variant and bold: the hue, and so what it says, stays.
This is the one mapping, in the library, and the colours are given by
.BR tui_table_set_colors ():
256 colours use background 17, 16 colours blue, and 8 colours no
background at all (the
.RB \(dq > \(dq
and bold carry the selection).
.BR tui_table_init ()
chooses from
.B COLORTERM
and
.BR TERM .
The styles of the header and of the selection are fields of the table.
.PP
.BR tui_table_render ()
draws the header (and, with
.BR TUI_TABLE_RULER ,
a row of
.RB \(dq \(eq \(dq
under it), then the rows from the top of the viewport, in the canvas.
.BR tui_table_render_header ()
and
.BR tui_table_render_row ()
draw one of them only: a program that fetches its rows over the network
draws each when it arrives, and redraws one changed row without
repainting the table.
The widget never reads input.
.PP
The table owns a set of marked rows, for a batch action.
With
.B TUI_TABLE_MARKS
in
.IR flags ,
Space in
.BR tui_table_handle ()
toggles the mark of the cursor row and moves down one; without it the keys
are unchanged.
A marked row shows a bold
.RB \(dq * \(dq
beside the
.RB \(dq > \(dq
(it takes the place of the row's
.BR tui_table_row_mark ()
glyph while marked), in the row's colours, selected or not.
.BR tui_table_marked ()
asks one row, and
.BR tui_table_next_marked ()
walks the marked rows in index order, starting from \-1.
.BR tui_table_mark_all (),
.BR tui_table_invert_marks ()
and
.BR tui_table_clear_marks ()
act on the whole list; section rows are never marked.
.PP
The marks are kept by a row's identity, so they survive a reload, a sort
and a filter:
.BR tui_table_set_row_id ()
gives the callback that returns it (a UID, an issue number).
Without it the identity is the row index.
.BR tui_table_marked_count ()
is the size of the set, which with a row id also holds marked rows the list
does not hold at the moment.
.BR tui_table_set_marked ()
and
.BR tui_table_toggle_mark ()
return 0, or \-1 with
.B errno
EINVAL (no such row) or ENOMEM; so do
.BR tui_table_mark_all ()
and
.BR tui_table_invert_marks ().
.BR tui_table_free ()
releases the set; a program that never marks need not call it.
.PP
.BR tui_table_set_message ()
shows a text instead of the header and the rows: an empty list's
explanation, or the error that kept it from being read.
.BR tui_table_set_total ()
is the number of rows the program has in all, for a window of a larger set;
.BR tui_table_first_visible ()
and
.BR tui_table_last_visible ()
give the range on the screen for a title's \(dqa\-b of N\(dq.
.PP
.BR tui_table_format_row ()
and
.BR tui_table_format_header ()
give the same columns as plain text, for a list printed to a pipe;
.I cols
of 0 or less means no cut and no padding, the full strings.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_table_handle ()
returns 1 when the key was a movement key, 0 otherwise.
.BR tui_table_selected ()
returns the row, or \-1.
.BR tui_table_widths ()
returns the number of columns shown and sets the width of each.
.BR tui_table_render_row ()
returns 1 when the row was drawn, 0 when it is not in view.
The format functions return the bytes written, or \-1 when
.I outsz
was too small.
.SH SEE ALSO
